Acknowledgement of NOAA-GFDL
----------------------------

The numerical methods in Roundabout were developed by NOAA's Geophysical
Fluid Dynamics Laboratory (NOAA-GFDL) and the developers of MOM6 and SIS2.
Roundabout's own contribution is adapting those methods to GPUs: the
data-parallel loops are written with Fortran `do concurrent`, and OpenACC is
used for data movement and reductions. The ocean dynamical core follows MOM6,
which is also Roundabout's physics oracle (the reference its answers are
checked against). The sea-ice component follows SIS2.

Roundabout is a hobby project, written for fun and as the author's
learning project. The author is part of MOM6-GPU
(https://github.com/MOM6-GPU), the effort to port MOM6 to GPUs.
Roundabout runs in parallel to, and separately from, that work and the
GPU porting efforts at NOAA-GFDL and ACCESS-NRI, and it is not part of
them.

This repository contains code ported from and modified from the following
NOAA-GFDL projects. Both are licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0,
and a copy of that licence is in LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt.

  MOM6, the Modular Ocean Model version 6
    https://github.com/NOAA-GFDL/MOM6
    Ported and adapted: the split-explicit barotropic solver, continuity-PPM,
    the finite-volume pressure gradient, ALE remapping, the vertical and
    lateral closures, and the vertical-friction / bottom-boundary-layer
    treatment. tools/om_topo_limit.f90 is derived from MOM6 code.

  SIS2, the Sea Ice Simulator version 2
    https://github.com/NOAA-GFDL/SIS2
    Ported and adapted: the sea-ice thermodynamics, the ice-thickness
    distribution, the category transport, the C-grid elastic-viscous-plastic
    (EVP) rheology and the ice-ocean coupling.

These files were modified from the originals: they were translated into
Roundabout's data layout and GPU programming model, and in places changed
further. The deliberate differences from MOM6 and SIS2 are documented in
docs/CLOSURE_MATRIX.md, docs/CAPABILITIES_AND_LIMITATIONS.md and the module
docstrings.

